    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The glucose-6-phosphatase enzyme removes the phosphate group from glucose-6-phosphate, resulting in the formation of free glucose. This glucose is then transported out of the liver cell and into the bloodstream.\n\nThe transport of glucose out of the liver cell is facilitated by a process called facilitated diffusion, which involves a family of transport proteins called glucose transporter type 2 (GLUT2). GLUT2 is a transmembrane protein that spans the cell membrane, with one end embedded in the cytosol and the other end exposed to the bloodstream.\n\nWhen glucose-6-phosphate is formed, it is transported across the cell membrane by GLUT2, where it is converted back to glucose by the enzyme glucose-6-phosphatase. The resulting glucose is then released into the bloodstream, where it can be taken up by other cells or used by the body for energy production.\n\nIt's worth noting that the glucose released into the bloodstream is not immediately available for use by the body. Instead, it is released into the bloodstream in a form that is not immediately usable by cells, such as glucose-6-phosphate. This glucose-6-phosphate is then transported to other cells, where it can be converted back to glucose-6-phosphate",
